const bodyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Circulatory & respiratory', note: 'The delivery service: heart pumps ~5 L/min; lungs load O₂ and unload CO₂ across membranes a cell thick. Together they keep every cell supplied within seconds.' },
    { math: '2 · Nervous & endocrine', note: 'Two control networks: nerves (milliseconds, targeted signals) and hormones (seconds–hours, broadcast through blood). Reflexes, thoughts, growth and puberty all run on this pairing.' },
    { math: '3 · Digestive & excretory', note: 'Fuel processing: a 9-metre tube dismantles food into molecules; the kidneys filter ~180 L of blood daily, reclaiming what\'s worth keeping and flushing the rest.' },
    { math: '4 · Immune, skeletal, muscular, integumentary, reproductive', note: 'Defence, frame, motion, wrapping and the next generation. Eleven systems total — none works alone: running requires nerves, muscles, lungs, heart and bones in the same second.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/human-body-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The human body is <strong>eleven interlocking systems built from ~37 trillion
    cells</strong>, each system a specialist service for the whole. The unifying theme is
    <strong>homeostasis</strong>: keeping temperature, pH, sugar and salt within narrow ranges
    despite a chaotic world. Health is homeostasis holding; disease is it slipping; medicine is
    mostly helping it back.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the systems, grouped by what they do for the whole" widget="equationStep" config={bodyCfg} />

  <h2>Homeostasis in action</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Thermoregulation:</strong> sweat when hot, shiver when cold — a thermostat with a 1 °C tolerance, run by the hypothalamus.</li>
    <li><strong>Blood sugar:</strong> insulin lowers, glucagon raises — two hormones in a push–pull loop. Diabetes is this loop failing; the treatment (insulin, 1922) is one of medicine's great saves.</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback loops everywhere:</strong> negative loops stabilise (most of the body); positive loops drive events to completion (childbirth, blood clotting). Physiology is loop-reading.</li>
    <li><strong>You are outnumbered:</strong> ~38 trillion bacteria live on and in you — the microbiome aids digestion, trains the immune system, and even influences mood. The "you" being kept alive is an ecosystem.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Anatomy and Physiology 2e; Guyton & Hall (clinical reference); Sender, Fuchs & Milo, PNAS (2016) on cell counts.</p>
</TopicPage>
